 ],
 "literal": "Strong-charioted Nalli's forest cowherds' / many cows yielded ghee-with Tondi's / completely grown white rice hot rice / in seven vessels even if offered, is small, my friend, / large shoulders made-thin distress-for / guest to come cawed crow's offering indeed.",
 "translation": "Even if we offer hot white rice, grown completely in the fields of Tondi and rich with the ghee yielded by the many cows of the forest-dwelling cowherds of the strong-charioted chieftain Nalli, served in seven large vessels, it would still be too small an offering for the crow that cawed to announce the arrival of our guest, curing the distress that made your large shoulders grow thin.",
 "modern": "வலிமையான தேரை உடைய நள்ளியின் காட்டில் வாழும் இடையர்களின் பல பசுக்கள் தந்த நெய்யினைக் கொண்ட தொண்டித் துறையின் முழுமையாக விளைந்த வெண்ணெல்லின் சூடான சோற்றை, ஏழு கலங்களில் ஏந்திப் படைத்தாலும், என் தோழியே! உன்னுடைய பெரிய தோள்களை மெலியச் செய்த துன்பம் நீங்குமாறு, விருந்தினர் வருவதை அறிவித்துக் கரைந்த காக்கைக்கு நாம் அளிக்கும் பலி மிகச் சிறியதே ஆகும்.",
 "explanation": "In this Mullai poem, the confidante speaks to the heroine upon the hero's return from his journey. She expresses her immense gratitude to the crow whose cawing heralded the arrival of the hero, thereby curing the heroine's deep distress that had caused her shoulders to waste away. The confidante declares that even an offering of seven large vessels filled with the finest hot white rice from the fertile region of Tondi, drenched in rich ghee from the forest herds of chieftain Nalli, would be too small a reward for this auspicious bird. This poem beautifully reflects the Mullai landscape's theme of patient waiting and the joyful relief of reunion. The poet Nacceḷḷaiyār famously earned the name 'Kākkai Pāṭiṉiyār' (the crow-singer) due to this vivid and celebrated portrayal of the crow as a messenger of hope.",
